0.30 & 9.85 & $[6.70,\; 16.29]$ & ${<}\,0.001$ \\ % exp_id: deepseek_bf16_permtest_s030; source: permutation_test_results.json s030.permutation_test_difference
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The monotonic growth of both the point estimate and the CI lower bound ($0.19 \to 0.69 \to 1.66 \to 6.70$) confirms that compound severity amplification under importance-based eviction strengthens with sparsity---not merely in central tendency but also in worst-case lower bounds. % exp_id: deepseek_bf16_permtest; source: permutation_test_results.json

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig4_bootstrap.pdf}
\caption{Forest plot of permutation test results for the difference statistic. Horizontal bars show 95\% permutation CIs; diamonds mark observed median differences $\widetilde{\Delta}$. All CIs lie entirely above zero (vertical dashed line), confirming significant strategy-dependent severity at every sparsity level.}
\label{fig:permtest-forest}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Effect sizes.}
Table~\ref{tab:cohens-d} reports Cohen's $d$ (bootstrap $B{=}10{,}000$) measuring the standardized difference in II between low- and high-sparsity phases under importance-based eviction.

